---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getMeta } from '../lib/data.mjs';
const meta = getMeta();
const title = `Editorial & data-integrity policy — ${meta.title}`;
const description = `How StatuteRates sources and labels interest-rate records, how often automated series refresh, our calculator safety gate, corrections process, and data limits.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'Editorial & data-integrity policy',
  url: new URL('/editorial-policy/', Astro.site).href,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: meta.title },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin-bottom:6px">
    <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <span>Editorial policy</span>
  </nav>
  <h1>Editorial &amp; data-integrity policy</h1>
  <p class="lead">Every number on this site is a legal figure someone may rely on to calculate what they
    owe or are owed. That is why accuracy — not volume — is the point. Here is exactly how we verify each
    rate, how often we re-check it, and what we do when we get something wrong.</p>

  <h2>How rates are sourced</h2>
  <p>We record a citation and URL alongside every value. A controlling government source is preferred.
    State-authorized code portals and judicial summaries are labeled official-secondary when they are
    useful but are not themselves the controlling enactment. State rules remain calculator-ineligible
    until the source, history, branches, and arithmetic mechanics are complete. Records fall into four kinds:</p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li><strong>Statute-fixed rates</strong> (e.g. a flat 12% written into the code) are verified word-for-word
      against a cited statute or court source and re-checked on a schedule because a legislature can amend them.</li>
    <li><strong>Agency- or court-set variable rates</strong> (a prime-rate or Treasury-linked formula reset
      each quarter or year) are read from the official agency page that publishes the current period's
      figure, with the effective period recorded.</li>
    <li><strong>Derived rates</strong> (e.g. the federal post-judgment rate under 28 U.S.C. §1961, or Iowa's
      Treasury-plus-two formula) are computed by us from an official series using the governing statutory
      formula — and every such page shows the formula and links the underlying data, so you can check our
      arithmetic. We never present a computed number as authoritative without its formula and source.</li>
    <li><strong>Case-specific rules</strong> have no honest statewide numeric value. In those records the
      API deliberately returns a null number and a plain-language rule, preventing rankings and calculators
      from silently treating a contract rate or judge-selected rate as a universal percentage.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When a rate has a genuine carve-out — a different figure for tort versus contract, for consumer debt,
    or against a government entity — we state it rather than flattening it into one number, because the
    wrong branch is as misleading as the wrong number.</p>

  <h2>How often each series is re-checked</h2>
  <p>The derived and market-linked federal, UK, and EU series refresh <strong>automatically once a week</strong>
    from their official sources; a validation suite checks types, ranges, and cross-value consistency, and
    if anything fails the failed run cannot overwrite the last validated export. Curated state rates require
    a separate source-check process. Every page carries the <strong>effective date</strong> recorded for the
    value and exposes all observations currently held in the <a href="/api/">free JSON API</a>; a one-point
    record is not described as complete historical coverage.</p>

  <h2>Calculator safety gate</h2>
  <p>A state reference value is not automatically safe for calculation. State calculators stay disabled
    unless the source is primary and the rate history, claim-type branches, accrual rule, day-count rule,
    reset behavior, and compounding method are all structured and pass automated validation.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you believe a rate is wrong, email <a href="mailto:hello@statuterates.com">hello@statuterates.com</a>
    with the page and, if you can, the official source. We check every report against the controlling
    primary source. If we confirm an error we correct the record and its provenance; the
    <a href="/changes/">rate-change feed</a> reports newly recorded effective-rate changes, not a complete
    editorial correction ledger. Before reporting, it is worth
    opening the official source linked on the page — a rate may have changed at its statutory reset date
    since you last looked, which is exactly the kind of movement this site exists to track.</p>

  <h2>Independence &amp; funding</h2>
  <p>StatuteRates is independent and designed to be supported by display advertising, not by subscriptions, sponsors, or
    referral deals with any law firm or vendor. Advertising never influences a rate: the numbers come only
    from the cited sources, and the data and API are free and un-paywalled. If advertising is enabled, the provider may
    set cookies — see the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>Using &amp; citing the data</h2>
  <p>Statutes and court rules are government edicts; statistical publications and third-party pages may
    carry separate reuse terms, which are recorded per source. Our compilation, wording, and effective-date
    provenance are our own work. You are welcome to cite or link to any page, and every rate page includes
    a one-click <em>&ldquo;Cite this page&rdquo;</em> citation. For programmatic access, the
    <a href="/api/">JSON API</a> is free and needs no key. If you want to reuse the compiled dataset at
    scale, a short note to <a href="mailto:hello@statuterates.com">hello@statuterates.com</a> is appreciated.</p>

  <h2>What we are — and are not</h2>
  <p>We are a reference for what the statutory, judgment, prejudgment, and tax interest rates currently are,
    with sources you can verify. We are <strong>not</strong> a law firm and this is <strong>not</strong>
    legal, tax, or financial advice. Which rate applies to a specific matter — and how it compounds, when it
    starts, and what carve-outs apply — is a legal question for the controlling statute and, where needed, a
    licensed professional. Always confirm the figure against the official source before you rely on it.</p>

  <div class="disclaimer">
    Reference data only — not legal, tax, or financial advice. See our
    <a href="/methodology/">methodology &amp; sources</a>, <a href="/about/">about page</a>,
    <a href="/terms/">Terms &amp; Disclaimer</a>, and <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
